{
  "abstract": "Post-training quantization methods are designed and compared with a Hessian-weighted layer error measured on the calibration Hessian. Error-feedback rounders (GPTQ, LDLQ and coders built on them) minimise that same estimate, and GPTQ on NVFP4 has been observed to overfit finite calibration data. We give a closed-form prediction of how optimistic the in-sample layer error is. With the damped calibration Hessian factored as $(H_0+\\lambda\\bar d I)^{-1}=U^\\top U$ and zero-mean, uncorrelated, equal-variance rounding noise, error feedback leaves error rows with covariance $C=U^\\top\\mathrm{diag}(U_{jj}^{-2})U$, so the expected held-out/in-sample ratio is $\\mathrm{tr}(CH_t)/\\mathrm{tr}(CH_0)$ times a ratio of weight energies; round-to-nearest is the case $C=I$. Only two Hessians are needed. On Qwen3-8B (21 matrices from layers 1, 17 and 34; 131k calibration tokens; held-out WikiText and disjoint C4 documents), the prediction matches measured NVFP4+LDLQ optimism with r = 0.998 and 0.05 dB mean error. It holds with and without a Hadamard rotation and at damping 0.01–1.0 (mean errors at most 0.04 dB). With WikiText calibration the optimism is 0.8 dB on held-out WikiText and 2.3 dB on C4. It is about 1.7 dB for 12288-wide down_proj inputs vs 0.6 dB for 4096-wide inputs, within 0.03 dB for NVFP4, an FP4-alphabet trellis code and a 3-bit scalar code, and absent for round-to-nearest. Mixed-domain calibration (C4: 2.3 to 1.1 dB) and damping reduce it as predicted. The in-sample proxy misranked a 3.5-bit trellis code: it rated it 2.3 dB better than 4.5-bit NVFP4 round-to-nearest, held-out C4 gives 0.02 dB, and whole-model weight-only perplexity is worse (10.103/12.750 vs 9.871/12.486 on WikiText-2/C4). A second case, a scale-offset code accurate out of sample but 0.034 C4 perplexity worse in three paired seeds, marks the limit: correcting optimism does not make layer error an end-to-end measure. Limitations: one model, input-side Hessians only, simulated quantization.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Corrections requested in the v1 review; no new claims or results.\n1. Probabilistic scope: the formula is stated as a ratio of expected quadratic errors under a zero-mean, uncorrelated, equal-variance rounding-noise model (Sections 1, 3, abstract). U is defined from the damped Hessian throughout. The text says tr(CH_t) > tr(CH_0) is an empirical regularity here, not a guarantee. The supplement no longer calls it an \"exact correction\". Code independence is limited to the three codes tested (Sections 1, 2).\n2. Sampling vs distribution shift: WikiText draw 1 is described as a fresh window draw from the same corpus, not document-disjoint; only the C4 split is document-disjoint. Added that q/k/v and gate/up share Hessians (21 matrices = 12 covariance estimates), so the 42 cases are descriptive checks, not independent replications.\n3. Scale-offset example: the paired C4 differences are now reported (+0.020, +0.024, +0.058; mean +0.034, sample sd 0.021), replacing the baseline seed sd. Added the caveat that three seeds do not establish population-level degradation, and that fixed-temperature perplexity is an imperfect quality measure [15].\n4. Reproduction: Section 6 and the supplement README give the working directory, PYTHONPATH, baseline location, model/data arguments, exact Hessian-generation commands, and a claim-to-file map. The stale results index was removed.\n5. Related work: cites the rStation papers [14] (block-maximum profile) and [15] (temperature fitting). States that the error-feedback and trace identity are standard (QuIP [2], [13]); the contribution is the quantizer-free magnitude prediction and its validation.",
